  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposal would result in the loss of an existing family-sized dwelling and its replacement with smaller one and two-bedroom units and studios, without providing a replacement family-sized home. The development would therefore fail to provide an appropriate dwelling mix or retain family accommodation to meet identified local housing needs, contrary to Policy H10 of the London Plan (2021) and Policies DMD 3 and DMD 5 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development would provide a poor standard of accommodation for future occupiers due to inadequate internal floorspace within Flats 1, 2 and 4; the restricted outlook and inadequate natural ventilation available to Bedroom 2 of Flat 1; the absence of private outdoor amenity space for Flats 2, 3 and 4; and the constrained access, limited daylight, poor outlook and lack of privacy associated with Flat 4. Collectively, these shortcomings would result in cramped, contrived and substandard accommodation that would fail to meet the day-to-day needs of future occupiers, contrary to Policy D6 of the London Plan (2021) and Policies DMD 8 and DMD 9 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed first-floor rear extension, by reason of its excessive width, scale and form, would create an awkward relationship with the host building and result in a cramped, cluttered and ad hoc rear elevation. It would fail to respect the established rear building pattern, architectural coherence and group value of Sykes' Buildings, causing unjustified harm to the significance of this locally listed non-designated heritage asset. The identified harm would not be outweighed by the benefits of the development. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policies D3, D4 and HC1 of the London Plan (2021), Core Policy 31 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), Policies DMD 37 and DMD 44 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014), and Section 20 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026).",
    "The proposed shopfront alterations, including the loss of the recessed entrance, narrower doorway and changes to the shop display window proportions, would create a cramped and unsympathetic frontage. Insufficient details of materials, finishes and construction have also been provided. The proposal would cause unjustified harm to the character and significance of the locally listed Sykes' Buildings, contrary to London Plan Policies D4 and HC1, Core Policy 31, DMD Policies 37, 40 and 44, and Section 20 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2026).",
    "In the absence of evidence to demonstrate that there is sufficient capacity to accommodate the parking demand, the proposed development would increase car parking pressure within the vicinity of the site, contrary to Policies DMD 5 and DMD 45 of the Development Management Document (2014) and Policies T6 and T6.1 of the London Plan (2021)."
  ],
